<Project>

  <!--
    Benchmark harness code is not the subject of the coverage report, exactly
    like the test tree (see test/Directory.Build.props). The throughput rig
    (azure-throughput Silo/Producer) and the BenchmarkDotNet hosts under
    benchmark/host are deploy-and-run-by-hand measurement scaffolding, not
    product surface the README coverage badge advertises. Counting them would
    distort the product coverage number.

    Emitting [assembly: ExcludeFromCodeCoverage] via the SDK's AssemblyAttribute
    item (GenerateAssemblyInfo is on by default) marks every assembly under
    benchmark/ so coverlet's "XPlat Code Coverage" collector skips it if it is
    ever loaded by a test host, without a per-project edit. This inherits into
    all benchmark projects beneath this directory.

    The attribute is only emitted for target frameworks whose
    ExcludeFromCodeCoverageAttribute permits assembly-level use: assembly support
    was added in .NET Core 3.0 (AttributeTargets.Assembly). On netstandard2.0 and
    .NET Framework the attribute is class/method-only, so an [assembly: ...]
    application is a CS0592 compile error. Every benchmark project targets
    net10.0 today, so the guard is defensive - it keeps this props file safe if a
    netstandard2.0/.NET Framework helper is ever added under benchmark/, and
    matches samples/Directory.Build.props (which does host such a project). The
    condition lives in a target (not a top-level ItemGroup) because
    $(TargetFramework) is not yet resolved when this props file is imported; a
    per-TFM inner build sets it before the target runs.
  -->
  <Target Name="_ExcludeBenchmarkFromCodeCoverage"
          BeforeTargets="CoreGenerateAssemblyInfo"
          Condition="'$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETCoreApp' Or ('$(TargetFrameworkIdentifier)' == '.NETStandard' And '$(TargetFrameworkVersion)' == 'v2.1')">
    <ItemGroup>
      <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.ExcludeFromCodeCoverage" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
